      "title": "Using the Sum and Constant Multiple Rules for Differentiation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "f5989545-304a-5019-90ca-bebab05051f2",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Find $(2\\mathbf{f}(t)-2\\mathbf{g}(t))^{\\prime}\\big|_{t=1}$, where $t>-3$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ \\cos{\\left(t - 1 \\right)} \\\\ - 4 \\sqrt{t + 3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\mathbf{g}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}\\left(t + 1\\right)^{2} \\\\ - 2 t^{2} \\\\ 6 - 4 t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Apply the **sum and constant multiple rules**:\n\n$$\n(2\\mathbf{f}(t)-2\\mathbf{g}(t))^{\\prime}=2\\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}(t)-2\\mathbf{g}^{\\prime}(t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ - \\sin{\\left(t - 1 \\right)} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t + 3}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\mathbf{g}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}2 t + 2 \\\\ - 4 t \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow evaluate the derivatives at $t=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}(1)&=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\mathbf{g}^{\\prime}(1)&=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nScale every component by its constant multiplier:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}(1)&=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ -2\\mathbf{g}^{\\prime}(1)&=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd corresponding components and combine like terms:\n\n$$\n(2\\mathbf{f}(t)-2\\mathbf{g}(t))^{\\prime}\\big|_{t=1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nKeep the displayed inner arguments when applying the scalar derivative rules; any nonunit inner derivative multiplies its component.",
